  "full_response": "Certainly! Below is a sample README that includes example `curl` commands demonstrating various ways to authenticate API requests, such as using Bearer tokens, API keys included in headers, and Basic Authentication credentials.\n\n---\n\n# Project Name\n\nA short description of your project, its purpose, and key functionalities.\n\n## Getting Started\n\nInstructions on how to set up and use your project.\n\n## API Usage Examples\n\nThis section provides examples of how to interact with the API using `curl`.\n\n### Using Bearer Tokens\n\nBearer tokens are used to authenticate requests. The bearer token should be included in the `Authorization` header.\n\nExample:\n\n```bash\ncurl -X GET \"https://api.example.com/v1/resources\" \\\n     -H \"Authorization: Bearer YOUR_BEARER_TOKEN\"\n```\n\nReplace `YOUR_BEARER_TOKEN` with your actual bearer token.\n\n### Using API Keys in Headers\n\nSome APIs require an API key to be sent in the headers for request authentication.\n\nExample:\n\n```bash\ncurl -X POST \"https://api.example.com/v1/resources\" \\\n     -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\n     -H \"x-api-key: YOUR_API_KEY\" \\  # Replace YOUR_API_KEY with your actual API key\n     -d '{\"key1\":\"value1\",\"key2\":\"value2\"}'\n```\n\n### Using Basic Authentication\n\nBasic Authentication involves encoding the username and password in Base64 and including it in the `Authorization` header.\n\nExample:\n\n```bash\ncurl -X GET \"https://api.example.com/v1/resources\" \\\n     -u \"username:password\" \\  # Replace username and password with your credentials\n     -H \"Authorization: Basic $(echo -n 'username:password' | base64)\"\n```\n\nEnsure that you replace `username` and `password` with your actual credentials for Basic Authentication.\n\n## Response Handling\n\nInformation on how to handle responses, including error messages and possible status codes.\n\n## Additional Resources\n\nLinks to further documentation, a changelog, or other resources related to the project.\n\n---\n\nReplace `https://api.example.com/v1/resources` with the actual endpoints you are targeting, and make sure to replace any placeholders (e.g., `YOUR_BEARER_TOKEN`, `YOUR_API_KEY`, `username`, `password`) with your own credentials or tokens.\n\nFor a production environment, ensure that tokens and credentials are handled securely to prevent unauthorized access. Consider using environment variables or secret management tools to keep sensitive information protected.",
